
# Test Suite (ESP32 On-Board)

This repository ships a GoogleTest-powered suite that runs directly on the ESP32-S3 dev kit. It validates the helper utilities used to parse LD2450 radar frames, so contributors can refactor logic without connecting the physical sensor.

## Contents

- `test_onboard/test_ld2450_radar.cpp` boots an Arduino sketch, calls `RUN_ALL_TESTS()` inside `loop()`, and exercises the helper layer.
- `test_onboard/SerialMock.h` provides a `Stream` mock using GoogleMock so we can feed deterministic serial data.

## Covered Scenarios

- Vector helpers keep packet fragments ordered (`concatenate_vectors`).
- Serial ingestion stops at protocol markers and discards trailing bytes (`read_until`).
- Little-endian conversions are reversible for 16-bit payload fields (`to_little_endian`, `from_little_endian`).
- Byte pattern lookups catch sync markers inside buffers (`contains`).
- Unsigned-to-signed translation matches the radar's direction encoding (`to_signed`).

Treat this list as a living contract; extend it whenever helper behavior grows.

## Running the Suite

1. Plug the ESP32-S3 board into USB (sensor optional; serial data is mocked).
2. From the project root run:

```bash
pio test -e test-onboard
```

PlatformIO flashes the test binary, attaches to the configured `monitor_speed` (115200 baud), and streams GoogleTest output.

### Tips

- If auto-detection fails, pass the port manually: `pio test -e test-onboard --upload-port /dev/tty.usbserial-*`.
- Keep `monitor_speed` in `platformio.ini` aligned with your board to avoid garbled logs.

## Contributing Tests

- Use `SerialMock` plus `ON_CALL` expectations to script new byte streams.
- Group helper-focused assertions under the existing `Helpers` test fixture for readability.
- Add short notes here whenever new helpers or protocols gain coverage so GitHub readers know what the suite guarantees.

## Notes

- This README section was drafted with AI assistance for documentation clarity.
- The underlying test cases (`test_onboard/test_ld2450_radar.cpp`) remain hand-crafted and reviewed by the maintainers.